+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: euclid
+
+Property-based tests written with `hegeltest` v0.28.2 (library crate name: `hegel`),
+added as a dev-dependency with `cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version`
+(euclid pins `rust-version = 1.63.0`; hegeltest's MSRV is 1.86 — a maintainer
+decision if these tests are upstreamed).
+
+Tests live in the existing in-source test modules of the files they test, in
+nested `mod hegel_props` blocks:
+
+- `src/angle.rs` (4 tests)
+- `src/transform2d.rs` (2 tests)
+- `src/rotation.rs` (2 tests)
+- `src/box2d.rs` (5 tests)
+- `src/point.rs` (1 test)
+
+## Properties tested
+
+| # | Test | Property | Evidence |
+|---|------|----------|----------|
+| 1 | `angle::float::hegel_props::hegel_positive_is_in_zero_two_pi` | `Angle::positive()` returns radians in `[0, 2*PI)` | Doc comment: "Returns this angle in the [0..2*PI[ range." |
+| 2 | `angle::float::hegel_props::hegel_signed_is_in_neg_pi_pi` | `Angle::signed()` returns radians in `]-PI, PI]` | Doc comment: "Returns this angle in the ]-PI..PI] range." |
+| 3 | `angle::float::hegel_props::hegel_angle_to_is_finite_for_finite_angles` | `angle_to` of two finite angles is finite | Doc comment: "Returns the shortest signed angle between two angles." An angle is not NaN. |
+| 4 | `angle::float::hegel_props::hegel_angle_to_magnitude_is_at_most_pi` | `angle_to` result magnitude is at most PI | "Shortest signed angle" — the shortest way around a circle never exceeds a half-turn. |
+| 5 | `transform2d::test::hegel_props::hegel_then_inverse_approximates_identity` | For well-conditioned invertible transforms, `t.then(&t.inverse()) ≈ identity` (eps 1e-6) | `inverse()` doc: "Returns the inverse transform if possible"; existing unit tests `test_inverse_scale`/`test_inverse_translate` check specific instances. |
+| 6 | `transform2d::test::hegel_props::hegel_transform_point_inverse_roundtrip` | `t⁻¹(t(p)) ≈ p` for well-conditioned transforms | Same contract, exercised at the point level. |
+| 7 | `rotation::tests::hegel_props::hegel_rotation2d_preserves_vector_length` | `Rotation2D::transform_vector` preserves vector length (relative eps 1e-12), for any finite angle | A rotation is an isometry by definition; `sin_cos` output is bounded for all finite inputs. |
+| 8 | `rotation::tests::hegel_props::hegel_rotation3d_inverse_roundtrip` | For a normalized quaternion, `q.inverse()` undoes `q.transform_vector3d` | `inverse()` returns the conjugate; `normalize()`/`is_normalized` document the unit-quaternion precondition (also `debug_assert`ed in `transform_vector3d`). |
+| 9 | `box2d::tests::hegel_props::hegel_box_rect_box_roundtrip_is_identity` | `Box2D -> Rect -> Box2D` is the exact identity for integer coordinates (incl. negative boxes) | `to_rect`/`to_box2d` are pure inverse arithmetic (`max - min` vs `origin + size`). |
+| 10 | `box2d::tests::hegel_props::hegel_intersects_iff_intersection_is_some` | `a.intersects(&b) == a.intersection(&b).is_some()` | `intersection()` doc: "returning None if the boxes do not intersect" — a direct consistency claim tying it to `intersects()`. |
+| 11 | `box2d::tests::hegel_props::hegel_intersection_is_contained_in_both` | A `Some` intersection is non-empty and contained in both operands | Meaning of set intersection; `intersection()` filters empty results through `to_non_empty` logic. |
+| 12 | `box2d::tests::hegel_props::hegel_union_contains_both_operands` | `a.union(&b)` contains both `a` and `b` | `union()` doc: "If either of the boxes is empty, the other one is returned"; `contains_box()` doc defines empty-box containment. |
+| 13 | `box2d::tests::hegel_props::hegel_from_points_contains_all_points_inclusive` | Every point given to `from_points` satisfies `contains_inclusive` on the result | Explicitly documented on `from_points` ("...are when probed with contains_inclusive"), including infinite coordinates. |
+| 14 | `point::point2d::hegel_props::hegel_lerp_is_exact_at_endpoints` | `p.lerp(q, 0.0) == p` and `p.lerp(q, 1.0) == q` exactly for finite points | The `lerp` doc examples assert exact equality at t = 0 and t = 1; the `(1-t)*a + t*b` formulation makes this exact. |
+
+Generator notes (per the skill's generator discipline):
+
+- Float generators are wide: full finite f64 range wherever the property allows
+  it, with explicit boosts (`one_of!`/`sampled_from`) toward boundary regions
+  (tiny negatives, values adjacent to PI, `f64::MAX`). NaN is excluded only
+  where comparisons are inherently meaningless (documented in-line); infinity
+  is kept where the operation should tolerate it (e.g. `from_points`).
+- Integer box coordinates are the full i32 range widened to i64, so the
+  library's internal arithmetic cannot overflow while magnitudes stay huge.
+  Boxes are frequently degenerate (zero width/height) to probe empty-box edges.
+- Transform components are drawn from [-100, 100] with a well-conditionedness
+  `assume` (`|det| > 1e-3 * s²`) — near-singular matrices have unboundedly
+  ill-conditioned inverses, so this protects the fixed tolerance, not a
+  library contract (comments in the tests say so).
+
+## Test results
+
+Final run (default case counts): **291 passed; 3 failed** — the three failures
+are deterministic pins of real bugs (see below), reproduced on 3/3 consecutive
+runs with fresh seeds and a cleared `.hegel/` database.
+
+Run-and-Reflect checks performed:
+
+- **Liveness**: temporarily inverted the assertion in
+  `hegel_union_contains_both_operands`; hegel reported a shrunk counterexample
+  (union of two empty boxes). Reverted.
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all hegel tests were run once at 10x case counts
+  (1000, and 3000 for the pinned tests). No new failures beyond the three
+  pinned bugs; the passing 11 properties stayed green.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+All three are pinned by deterministically-failing tests (boosted generators +
+raised `test_cases`), marked `KNOWN FAILURE` in the source. Per instructions,
+none were fixed.
+
+### 1. `Angle::positive()` can return exactly 2*PI, outside its documented range
+
+- **Test**: `hegel_positive_is_in_zero_two_pi` (src/angle.rs)
+- **Minimal counterexample** (hegel-shrunk): `radians = -2.220446049250313e-16`
+  → `positive()` returns `6.283185307179586` (= the crate's `two_pi`), violating
+  the documented `[0..2*PI[` half-open range.
+- **Code path**: `Angle::positive()` in `src/angle.rs` — after `a = self.radians % two_pi`,
+  the `a < 0` fixup computes `a + two_pi`, which rounds to exactly `two_pi`
+  whenever `|a| < ulp(2*PI)/2 ≈ 4.44e-16`.
+- **Knock-on effect**: through `signed()` (which is `pi - (pi - self).positive()`),
+  inputs just above PI (e.g. `f64::from_bits(PI.to_bits() + 1)`) yield exactly
+  `-PI`, violating `signed()`'s documented `]-PI..PI]` range. The sibling test
+  `hegel_signed_is_in_neg_pi_pi` excludes this known-buggy region (with a
+  comment referencing the canonical test) and passes on everything else.
+
+### 2. `Angle::angle_to()` returns NaN for finite angles (overflow → NaN)
+
+- **Test**: `hegel_angle_to_is_finite_for_finite_angles` (src/angle.rs)
+- **Minimal counterexample** (hegel-shrunk):
+  `from = 7.976931348622924e307`, `to = -1.0000000000000465e308`
+  → `angle_to` returns NaN.
+- **Code path**: `Angle::angle_to()` in `src/angle.rs` —
+  `let d = (to.radians - self.radians) % max;`. When the two finite angles are
+  more than `f64::MAX` apart, the subtraction overflows to infinity and
+  `inf % two_pi` is NaN, which then propagates. This is the same
+  overflow-at-extremes pattern seen in sibling geometry crates.
+- **Sibling**: `hegel_angle_to_magnitude_is_at_most_pi` bounds inputs to
+  ±8.9e307 (so the difference cannot overflow, comment references the pin) and
+  verifies the shortest-angle bound everywhere else — it passes.
+
+### 3. `Box2D::intersects()` and `Box2D::intersection()` disagree on empty boxes
+
+- **Test**: `hegel_intersects_iff_intersection_is_some` (src/box2d.rs)
+- **Minimal counterexample** (hegel-shrunk):
+  `a = Box2D((0, 0), (2, 2))`, `b = Box2D((1, 1), (1, 1))` (an empty,
+  degenerate box strictly inside `a`):
+  `a.intersects(&b)` is `true`, but `a.intersection(&b)` is `None`.
+- **Code path**: `Box2D::intersects()` uses four strict comparisons that all
+  hold for an empty box strictly inside another box, while
+  `Box2D::intersection()` computes `intersection_unchecked` and returns `None`
+  because the result `Box2D((1,1),(1,1))` `is_empty()`. `intersection()`'s doc
+  ("returning None if the boxes do not intersect") makes the two APIs
+  contradict each other; geometrically an empty box intersects nothing, which
+  suggests `intersects()` is the one in the wrong.
+- **Siblings**: `hegel_intersection_is_contained_in_both` and
+  `hegel_union_contains_both_operands` pass, including for degenerate boxes.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **`positive()` idempotence** (`positive(positive(x)) == positive(x)`): not
+  written as a separate test — it fails only via bug #1 (positive(2*PI) = 0),
+  so it would be a second pin of the same root cause rather than a new
+  property.
+- **`lerp(t)` stays within the endpoints' bounding box for `t ∈ [0, 1]`**: not
+  asserted. The `(1-t)*a + t*b` formulation can exceed `max(a, b)` by an ulp
+  through ordinary rounding, so the strict bound is not a contract the code
+  ever promised; only the documented endpoint exactness (property 14) was kept.
+- **Exact `Rect` ↔ `Box2D` roundtrip for floats**: unsound as stated —
+  `(origin + size) - origin != size` in float arithmetic. Kept as an exact
+  property for integer coordinates instead (property 9).
+- **Strict `]-PI, PI]` bound for `signed()` on the full domain**: sound as a
+  spec, but currently violated via bug #1; the test keeps the strict bound and
+  excludes only the known-buggy inputs rather than weakening the assertion.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The skill's workflow worked well end-to-end; the "probe extremes" advice and
+  the boundary-conjunction note directly produced bugs #1 and #2 (the
+  `positive()` failure needs `|x| < 4.4e-16` and negative; the `angle_to`
+  failure needs two huge values of opposite sign — both found immediately with
+  boosted `one_of!` branches, and #2's shrunk counterexample would have been
+  unlikely from uniform generation alone).
+- The Rust reference was accurate throughout (package-vs-crate naming, MSRV
+  `--ignore-rust-version` note, `one_of!`, plain `&TestCase` helper functions,
+  `compose!` for an inline point generator). No stale API names encountered.
+- One small gap: the skill's "deterministically pinned" guidance says to boost
+  the generator and raise `test_cases`, but gives no target failure
+  probability. A rule of thumb (e.g. "the boosted branch should hit the buggy
+  region in ≥10% of cases so 300 cases make a miss astronomically unlikely")
+  would make the determinism requirement more actionable.
+- The failure-database note (gotcha #11) was useful: after the deliberate
+  liveness-check failure, `.hegel/` had recorded the inverted-assertion
+  counterexample and needed `rm -rf .hegel` to avoid confusing replays.
+  `.hegel/` was added to `.gitignore` up front as advised.
diff --git a/src/angle.rs b/src/angle.rs
index 8cda48f..7db189f 100644
--- a/src/angle.rs
+++ b/src/angle.rs
@@ -399,4 +399,127 @@ mod float {
         let sum = A::radians(6.0);
         assert_eq!(angles.iter().sum::<A>(), sum);
     }
+
+    #[cfg(test)]
+    mod hegel_props {
+        use super::Angle;
+        use hegel::generators;
+
+        const PI: f64 = core::f64::consts::PI;
+        const TWO_PI: f64 = 2.0 * core::f64::consts::PI;
+
+        /// Finite angles, boosted toward the boundary regions where wrapping
+        /// bugs live: tiny negatives, values adjacent to +/-PI and +/-2*PI,
+        /// and the extremes of the finite range. NaN and infinity are excluded
+        /// because wrapping an angle only makes sense for finite values
+        /// (`Angle::is_finite` exists for callers to check).
+        fn draw_angle(tc: &hegel::TestCase) -> f64 {
+            tc.draw(hegel::one_of!(
+                generators::floats::<f64>()
+                    .allow_nan(false)
+                    .allow_infinity(false),
+                generators::floats::<f64>()
+                    .min_value(-4e-16)
+                    .max_value(-1e-300),
+                generators::sampled_from(vec![
+                    -1e-20,
+                    -5e-324,
+                    PI,
+                    -PI,
+                    f64::from_bits(PI.to_bits() + 1), // smallest f64 above PI
+                    TWO_PI,
+                    -TWO_PI,
+                    f64::MAX,
+                    -f64::MAX,
+                ]),
+            ))
+        }
+
+        // KNOWN FAILURE: real bug. `positive()` documents a result in the
+        // "[0..2*PI[" range, but for tiny negative inputs (e.g. -1e-20) the
+        // fixup `a = a + two_pi` rounds to exactly 2*PI, which is outside the
+        // documented half-open range. Code path: `Angle::positive()` in
+        // src/angle.rs (the `a + two_pi` branch when `a < 0`).
+        #[hegel::test(test_cases = 300)]
+        fn hegel_positive_is_in_zero_two_pi(tc: hegel::TestCase) {
+            let radians = draw_angle(&tc);
+            let p = Angle::radians(radians).positive().radians;
+            assert!(
+                (0.0..TWO_PI).contains(&p),
+                "positive({radians:e}) = {p:e} is not in [0, 2*PI)"
+            );
+        }
+
+        // `signed()` documents a result in the "]-PI..PI]" range.
+        //
+        // `signed()` is built on `positive()`, whose 2*PI rounding bug is
+        // pinned by `hegel_positive_is_in_zero_two_pi` (through `signed()` it
+        // returns exactly -PI for inputs just above PI). Exclude the inputs
+        // that hit that known bug so this test keeps checking everything else.
+        #[hegel::test]
+        fn hegel_signed_is_in_neg_pi_pi(tc: hegel::TestCase) {
+            let radians = draw_angle(&tc);
+            let m = (PI - radians) % TWO_PI;
+            tc.assume(!(m < 0.0 && m + TWO_PI == TWO_PI));
+            let s = Angle::radians(radians).signed().radians;
+            assert!(
+                -PI < s && s <= PI,
+                "signed({radians:e}) = {s:e} is not in ]-PI, PI]"
+            );
+        }
+
+        /// Finite angles, boosted toward huge magnitudes whose pairwise
+        /// difference overflows f64.
+        fn draw_extreme_angle(tc: &hegel::TestCase) -> f64 {
+            tc.draw(hegel::one_of!(
+                generators::floats::<f64>()
+                    .allow_nan(false)
+                    .allow_infinity(false),
+                generators::floats::<f64>()
+                    .min_value(1e308)
+                    .max_value(f64::MAX),
+                generators::floats::<f64>()
+                    .min_value(-f64::MAX)
+                    .max_value(-1e308),
+            ))
+        }
+
+        // KNOWN FAILURE: real bug (float overflow). `angle_to()` documents
+        // "the shortest signed angle between two angles", but when two finite
+        // angles are far enough apart, `to.radians - self.radians` overflows
+        // to infinity and `inf % two_pi` is NaN, so a NaN angle is returned
+        // for finite inputs. Code path: `Angle::angle_to()` in src/angle.rs
+        // (`let d = (to.radians - self.radians) % max`).
+        #[hegel::test(test_cases = 300)]
+        fn hegel_angle_to_is_finite_for_finite_angles(tc: hegel::TestCase) {
+            let from = draw_extreme_angle(&tc);
+            let to = draw_extreme_angle(&tc);
+            let d = Angle::radians(from).angle_to(Angle::radians(to)).radians;
+            assert!(
+                d.is_finite(),
+                "angle_to({from:e} -> {to:e}) = {d:e} is not finite"
+            );
+        }
+
+        // "Shortest signed angle" means the result's magnitude is at most PI.
+        //
+        // Inputs are bounded to +/-8.9e307 so that `to - from` cannot
+        // overflow; the overflow-to-NaN at the extremes is the known failure
+        // pinned by `hegel_angle_to_is_finite_for_finite_angles`.
+        #[hegel::test]
+        fn hegel_angle_to_magnitude_is_at_most_pi(tc: hegel::TestCase) {
+            let range = || {
+                generators::floats::<f64>()
+                    .min_value(-8.9e307)
+                    .max_value(8.9e307)
+            };
+            let from = tc.draw(range());
+            let to = tc.draw(range());
+            let d = Angle::radians(from).angle_to(Angle::radians(to)).radians;
+            assert!(
+                d.abs() <= PI,
+                "angle_to({from:e} -> {to:e}) = {d:e} exceeds PI in magnitude"
+            );
+        }
+    }
 }
diff --git a/src/box2d.rs b/src/box2d.rs
